    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new logic is placed sensibly (a `default` helper on `OwnerMapper` alongside the existing `membershipPoints`/`membershipLevel`/`hasEmail` helpers, and a domain predicate on `Localities`), and it correctly reuses `membershipLevel(owner)` rather than re-deriving the tier. But there's one structural inconsistency worth fixing:\n\n**Locality derivation is now triplicated (`OwnerMapper`).** `ownerSegment` reuses `membershipLevel(owner)` for the TIER half (good \u2014 matches the codebase idiom), but for the AREA half it re-derives the region inline with `CustomerCode.regionOf(owner.getCustomerCode())`. That exact expression already appears twice in the mapping annotations:\n- line 36\u201337 (`locality` target)\n- line 40\u201341 (`timezone` target, nested)\n\nSo this change makes it a third copy. The codebase established the pattern of extracting derived scalars into `default` helpers precisely to avoid this (`membershipPoints` is shared by the mapping, `rawMembershipLevel`, and `membershipLevel`). The idiomatic move here is to add a `default String locality(Owner owner)` helper wrapping `CustomerCode.regionOf(owner.getCustomerCode())` and call it from `ownerSegment` (and ideally from the `locality`/`timezone` mappings), so `ownerSegment` composes two helper methods symmetrically instead of one helper + one inlined derivation.\n\nMinor observations (not blocking on their own):\n- `Localities.isKnownRegion` answers \"is this a known region?\" by querying `REGION_POSTCODES`. The known-region set is encoded redundantly across `CITY_REGION`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`, and `REGION_POSTCODES`; keying off the postcode map is arbitrary relative to the method's name. It works, but a dedicated canonical region set (or a comment noting why the postcode map is the source of truth) would read more naturally.",
